this is a production of cornell  university  hello and thank you for choosing to see  my talk about the definition of genes  for  organelle specifically  plastomphylogenomics  the paper i'll be summarizing is now in  press and systematic biology  let me start by providing some  background  in an important paper in 2009 scott  edwards announced the advent of the  species tree paradigm in molecular  systematics  its central dogma was the insight from  population genetics going back to the  1980s  that gene trees are not species trees  the corollary to this dogma is that gene  trees will disagree with one another due  to drift  this leads to lineage sorting but  incongruence can only occur if the genes  are unlinked  if recombination has made them  independent of one another  it is recombination therefore that  defines the units of phylogeny as has  been recognized for many years  following hudson's work in the 80s and  90s showing the complexity of historical  relationships of genes due to  recombination  i termed these phylogenetic units  coalescent genes or c genes for short  a simplified example of hudson's inside  is shown here for a single locus with  four alleles  a to d with a gene tree shown  recombination between non-sister alleles  a and c  leads to a recombinant allele characters  one to eight trace the origin of the  five prime portion of the allele to the  a allele  whereas the three prime portion of the  allele shares a history with allele c  there are thus two different histories  two different c genes  created by recombination  in that example recombination split a  functionally defined gene into two c  genes with high enough recombination  every nucleotide of a gene may have a  different history  maybe a separate c gene at the other end  of the spectrum  as the rate of recombination decreases  multiple linked molecular genes share  the same history comprising a single c  gene with no recombination the entire  genome can be a single historical unit  a single c gene it is the amount of  recombination  not functional definitions that  determines the size of the c gene  both ends of this spectrum of  recombination represent key assumptions  for species tree estimation  under the multi-species coalescent as  stated recently by bravo at all  each c gene must represent a potentially  independent  single uniform estimate of the species  tree  so there must be free recombination  between c genes  and no recombination within a c gene  to recap then too much recombination  violates the assumption that a single  molecular gene  is a suitable c gene at the other  extreme  assuming that each gene in a  non-recombining block is a separate c  gene violates the other recombination  assumption of free recombination  which is to say the ability of the c  gene to provide an independent estimate  of the species tree  this brings me to organelle genomes  it has been widely accepted as i wrote  many years ago that with no effective  recombination the c  gene can be an entire genome as is the  case of organelle or genomes  this remains a mainstream assumption in  coalescent theory as seen in this recent  quote from magnus nordborg  that mitochondrial dna represents a  single locus  for coalescent purposes there are  dissenters however  a recent paper by a distinguished group  of systematists on rosid phylogeny using  whole plastome sequences  reported incongruence among  protein-coding genes  incongruence is a hallmark of  recombination as i've mentioned  so they hypothesize that incongruence  could be due to historical independence  of plastid genes  caused by recombination consequently  they  analyzed their data using msc methods  an overlapping group of authors  published a node in mpe with a very  provocative title  in it they made the case that  accumulating knowledge of organelle  biology has been ignored even willfully  swept under the rug  by molecular systemitis resulting in  misrepresentation  misconceptions and the misuse of  organelle genomes in phylogenetic  studies what are these misconceptions  first that plastoms are circular and  don't recombine  second that heteroplasmy is rare and can  be ignored  third that plastids are universally  strictly maternally inherited  and the first three of these together  contribute to the last one most critical  for phylogeny reconstruction  that the plastome is a single coalescent  gene  i disagree with the authors on that  conclusion so let's explore these issues  point by point  plastoms have indeed been represented as  circles and this convention  continues but there's been disagreement  about this for over 30 years mainly from  arnold bendich who calls circular  chloroplast genomes the grand delusion  for phylogeny reconstruction it does not  matter how the genes of a plasma  represented of course  but what does matter is recombination  bendaget's models require  considerable intermolecular  recombination but recombination  albeit intramolecular is also part of  the circular model going back to the  1970s  and 1980s so the ability  of the plastome to recombine has never  been disputed  but the key point is not that is that  not all recombination is  is relevant it must be what i call  historically effective  and this is something that many authors  have pointed out over the years  here for example is a quote from cecile  n a some recombination events do not  affect the gene tree or its branch  lengths  indeed she continues it is advantageous  to concatenate neighboring sites  that have the same history to see what  she means  here is the earlier example i showed but  this time instead of modeling a  recombination event between alleles a  and c  which are non-sisters on the tree we  follow a recombination event  between sister alleles a and b clearly  this has no effect on the topology  and this is exactly the situation when a  mutation occurs in a plastid within a  plant cell i show this here as a  plastometer within a green plastid  experiencing a mutation  transforming the green plastid to white  the alleles will be sisters on the  phylogeny and so recombination between  them  occurring within the plastid where the  mutation occurred  will not affect the phylogeny what a  mutation in a plastone within a nucleoid  within a plastid will do  is to make a homoplasmic cell  heteroplasmic  which brings me to the next alleged  misconception  clearly heteroplasmy is not rare it is a  necessary consequence of mutation and  recombination  this has not been swept under the rug  but heteroplasmy caused by mutation in a  plastid  does not involve historically  differentiated plastoms and does not  produce incongruence  this requires hybridization which brings  me to the next misconception  that plastids are universally strictly  maternally inherited  for hybridization to bring together  historically differentiated plastoms  both parents must transmit their  plastids  it has been known for over 30 years that  many angiosperms are potentially capable  of biparental transmission this classic  paper suggested 14 percent of genera and  around 20 percent of families  but by parental transmission though  necessary is not sufficient to produce  incongruence through recombination  for recombination to affect phylogeny  reconstruction historically  differentiated plastoms must be brought  together  once there it is likely that they will  recombine  but this requires two things first that  a cell be heteroplasmic  with plastids bearing historically  differentiated plastones  but as vaughn noted in 1981 vegetative  segregation into pure cell lines is so  fast that such heteroplasmy is a  transient phenomenon  this rapid sorting out is what leads to  many variegated leaf phenotypes  it also requires that historically  differentiated plastoms be brought  together in the same nucleoid within the  same plastid  and as magesi at all noted in 1985 this  requires plastid fusion  which is a very rare phenomenon  consequently as they hypothesize  chloroplast dna recombination is rare  because plastid  fusion is rare  so in my opinion the plastom is still  best thought of as a single c  gene historically effective  recombination if it occurs at all  is likely to fall at the far end of the  recombination spectrum  where there is risk of violating the key  assumption of free recombination between  c genes so how much of a problem is  violation of recombination assumptions  for msc methods  turning first to the too much  recombination violation  simulations by lanier and knowles in  2012 and by wang and liu  in 2016 suggested that msc methods are  robust to some within locus  recombination on the other hand wang and  liu tested the too little recombination  assumption  and found that this has major  consequences on the accuracy of  phylogeny reconstruction under the msc  gonsalves at all recommend breaking the  plastum into multiple genes  putitive c genes but defined  functionally ndhf rbcl etc  they thus focus on this unlikely and  lower impact risk  but they are unconcerned about this  highly probable  major impact risk as it turns out their  recommendations of msc methods over  concatenation is somewhat ironic  reason i say this is because there's  been a heated debate about c  gene definition edwards and colleagues  in song at all 2012 conducted an msc  analysis of 447 mammalian nuclear  transcripts averaging 3.1 kb  by concatenating exons from genes  averaging about 140 kb  springer and gates derided this as what  they called concatelescence  and called into question the  implementation of msc in general  referring to it as the gene tree  delusion  edwards had all defended the procedure  citing among other things the linear  null simulation study showing that some  recombination is not a fatal problem for  species tree inference  and they defended the entire msc  paradigm  the typical angiosperm plastum is about  the same size as the average mammalian  gene used by song at all  but is far more densely packed with  genes than those genes  are with exons consequently one would  expect less recombination between  plastid genes than between these nuclear  exons  particularly considering the low  historically effective recombination  rate as i've discussed  the arguments of gonsalves at all  clearly echoed those  of springer and gates they contend there  is too much recombination within what  others have defined as a single c  gene yet they cite edwards at all as the  authority of orthodox application  of msc theory that's ironic  given all of this i do not believe that  gonsalves at all have made a strong case  for the plastomas comprising multiple c  genes this brings me to the final topic  of the talk  the theory and practice of phylogenomics  gonsalves at all suggests that  acceptance of the plastom is a single c  gene not only is a misconception but is  still being employed to justify the  importance of nuclear datasets  but have nuclear genes ever needed  justification for inferring species  trees  i don't believe so clearly the nuclear  genome can be assumed to comprise a  large number of sea genes  the plastom has only been used because  of its desirable technical properties  high copy number and its ability to  produce a robust tree through  concatenation  so now that nuclear genes are readily  available the question that i think  should be asked  is in practice what should the role of  plastom sequences be for hypothesizing  species relationships  gonsalves all argue that it should be  sufficient to sequence some number of  plastid genes and use them directly not  by concatenation  which in the msc paradigm is an  admission that only a gene tree is being  constructed  but by using msc approaches i believe  this would bring the  wheel the field full circle back to the  days before the difference between gene  trees and species trees was understood  and appreciated in plant systematics  the alternative is some combination of  plastid sequences with nuclear sequences  concatenation as noted is not favored  under the msc paradigm  and treating individual plastome genes  as equivalent to nuclear genes ignores  their likely linkage again violating the  free recombination assumption  so in my opinion best practice should  follow theory  treat the plastum as equivalent to any  other single c gene  regardless of how robust a hypothesis  hypothesis it provides  and combine it with nuclear genes each  of which is also assumed to be a c  gene so i want to leave you with a final  thought  courtesy of the great baseball  philosopher yogi bera  in theory there is no difference between  theory and practice  but in practice there is in practice  many plant molecular systemitis continue  to invest a great deal of effort  in plastomphylogenies which if one  believes the theory  provides a single gene estimate of the  species tree  i ask you does that make sense  thank you a reminder that the paper  should soon be available in systematic  biology  i'd like to acknowledge a number of  people i'm grateful in particular to  daisy gonzalez  bob jansen and tracy reulman for raising  this interesting question and discussing  it with me  and to the many people who contributed  in one way or another to the paper  thanks again  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu

