 hypothesize  chloroplast dna recombination is rare  because plastid  fusion is rare  so in my opinion the plastom is still  best thought of as a single c  gene historically effective  recombination if it occurs at all  is likely to fall at the far end of the  recombination spectrum  where there is risk of violating the key  assumption of free recombination between  c genes so how much of a problem is  violation of recombination assumptions  for msc methods  turning first to the too much  recombination violation  simulations by lanier and knowles in  2012 and by wang and liu  in 2016 suggested that msc methods are  robust to some within locus  recombination on the other hand wang and  liu tested the too little recombination  assumption  and found that this has major  consequences on the accuracy of  phylogeny reconstruction under the msc  gonsalves at all recommend breaking the  plastum into multiple genes  putitive c genes but defined  functionally ndhf rbcl etc  they thus focus on this unlikely and  lower impact risk  but they are unconcerned about this  highly probable  major impact risk as it turns out their  recommendations of msc methods over  concatenation is somewhat ironic  reason i say this is because there's  been a heated debate about c  gene definition edwards and colleagues  in song at all 2012 conducted an msc  analysis of 447 mammalian nuclear  transcripts averaging 3.1 kb  by concatenating exons from genes  averaging about 140 kb  springer and gates derided this as what  they called concatelescence  and called into question the  implementation of msc in general  referring to it as the gene tree  delusion  edwards had all defended the procedure  citing among other things the linear  null simulation study showing that some  recombination is not a fatal problem for  species tree inference  and they defended the entire msc  paradigm  the typical angiosperm plastum is about  the same size as the average mammalian  gene used by song at all  but is far more densely packed with  genes than those genes  are with exons consequently one would  expect less recombination between  plastid genes than between these nuclear  exons  particularly considering the low  historically effective recombination  rate as i've discussed  the arguments of gonsalves at all  clearly echoed those  of springer and gates they contend there  is too much recombination within what  others have defined as a single c  gene yet they cite edwards at all as the  authority of orthodox application  of msc theory that's ironic  given all of this i do not believe that  gonsalves at all have made a strong case  for the plastomas comprising multiple c  genes this brings me to the final topic  of the talk  the theory and practice of phylogenomics  gonsalves at all suggests that  acceptance of the plastom is a single c  gene not only is a misconception but is  still being employed to justify the  importance of nuclear datasets  but have nuclear genes ever needed  justification for inferring species  trees  i don't believe so clearly the nuclear  genome can be assumed to comprise a  large number of sea genes  the plastom has only been used because  of its desirable technical properties  high copy number and its ability to  produce a robust tree through  concatenation  so now that nuclear genes are readily  available the question that i think  should be asked  is in practice what should the role of  plastom sequences be for hypothesizing  species relationships  gonsalves all argue that it should be  sufficient to sequence some number of  plastid genes and use them directly not  by concatenation  which in the msc paradigm is an  admission that only a gene tree is being  constructed  but by using msc approaches i believe  this would bring the  wheel the field full circle back to the  days before the difference between gene  trees and species trees was understood  and appreciated in plant systematics  the alternative is some combination of  plastid sequences with nuclear sequences  concatenation as noted is not favored  under the msc paradigm  and treating individual plastome genes  as equivalent to nuclear genes ignores  their likely linkage again violating the  free recombination assumption  so in my opinion best practice should  follow theory  treat the plastum as equivalent to any  other single c gene  regardless of how robust a hypothesis  hypothesis it provides  and combine it with nuclear genes each  of which is also assumed to be a c  gene so i want to leave you with a final  thought  courtesy of the great baseball  philosopher yogi bera  in theory there is no difference between  theory and practice  but in practice there is in practice  many plant molecular systemitis continue  to invest a great deal of effort  in plastomphylogenies which if one  believes the theory  provides a single gene estimate of the  species tree  i ask you does that make sense  thank you a reminder that the paper  should soon be available in systematic  biology  i'd like to acknowledge a number of  people i'm grateful in particular to  daisy gonzalez  bob jansen and tracy reulman for raising  this interesting question and discussing  it with me  and to the many people who contributed  in one way or another to the paper  thanks again  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu


